in @prism

in @optics
in @glass
in @nl
in @mirror/lens
in @mirror/lens/cli

# @mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi — the coherence-settling loop as a stage.
#
# Second of eight verb sub-stages forward-promised in
# docs/specs/cli-as-prism.md §3 (table at §3 + §3.1 shape sketch).
# Realizes the `command kintsugi` declaration in mirror.spec at the
# stage altitude — the substrate level where the named loop
# (focus -> project -> shift -> settle) drives one tournament round-trip.
#
# === Why kintsugi is a stage, not a resonator ===
#
# Per cli-as-prism §2.1: the TOURNAMENT itself is structurally a
# `resonator` per optical-keywords.md §1.5 — a closed loop with output
# coupler — but the CLI sub-stage that drives one iteration is a `stage`
# rather than a `resonator`. Each invocation is a chain through the five
# ops, not a self-referential round-trip. The resonator lives at the
# kintsugi-engine altitude; the CLI surface is the stage that fires one
# round-trip from outside.
#
# Per cli-as-prism §5.2 (where the constraint sang): the five operations
# map onto the tournament loop WITHOUT FORCING. `kintsugi.settle` IS one
# iteration. `kintsugi.focus` IS peeking the next move. `kintsugi.split`
# IS enumerating candidate fills. The tournament was already the same
# algebra at a smaller scale; the recursion just made the algebra
# visible at the shell.
#
# === Family placement ===
#
# Parent bench: @mirror/lens/cli (shards/mirror/lens/cli.mirror).
# Family root:  @mirror/lens    (shards/mirror/lens.mirror).
# Sibling stages:
#   @mirror/lens/cli/compile    — landed at 737330f (the build pipeline)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/shatter    — materialize AST -> text (next)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/bootstrap  — autopoietic phase machine (next)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/join       — peer-entry (Glanville's verb)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/reflect    — VSM System 5 view
#   @mirror/lens/cli/time       — Elm-style time travel
#   @mirror/lens/cli/crack      — third-state lifecycle
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi.mirror` declares
# `@mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi` and only that.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/cli-as-prism.md §3, §3.1 (shape sketch is
#               this stage's sketch literally),
#               §5.2 (where kintsugi sang),
#               §7 (default-op rule: kintsugi declares `default focus`),
#               docs/specs/optical-keywords.md §1.2 (stage),
#               §1.5 (resonator — the tournament engine altitude),
#               mirror.spec (`command kintsugi { arg spec: ~f =
#               ~f'./mirror.spec'; flag target: list(str) = [];
#               flag emit_shatter: bool = false }`).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-kintsugi-variety-io]] (Ashby
#                      requisite-variety; @fate tournament IS variety-
#                      maintenance — this stage drives one round),
#                      [[architecture-kintsugi-bias-lift]] (kintsugi
#                      bias = lift @code -> @mirror; substrate-pull as
#                      gradient),
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]
#                      (stage IS a sub-prism specialization),
#                      [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]]
#                      (file path matches namespace),
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]] (shift =
#                      basis transformation on the loss landscape).
# Related shards: lens/cli.mirror (parent bench, the produced-prism
#                 form the user types),
#                 lens/cli/compile.mirror (sibling — the prior
#                 sub-stage; same template, different operand naming),
#                 glass.mirror (imperfect / transparency / au carriers).

stage @mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi {
  # Per cli-as-prism §7: kintsugi declares `default focus` — the
  # standard read-default. Bare `mirror kintsugi` peeks the next
  # tournament move (no write). Settling a tournament iteration is a
  # real substrate write per the @fate variety-maintenance contract;
  # the user pays only for the explicit `settle`. Reading is free.
  default focus

  # The five operations the produced prism exposes on the kintsugi
  # sub-manifold (the tournament's working set). Each operand below
  # names the natural shape of the manifold's element at that op's
  # slot, per the spec §3.1 sketch convention and §2.1's per-verb
  # decisions:
  #   focus    target       — peek the next tournament candidate (no write)
  #   project  predicate    — filter the working set by what to repair
  #                          (target, eigenvalue range, transparency band)
  #   split    candidate    — enumerate candidate fills
  #   shift    basis        — basis-transform on the loss landscape,
  #                          per [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]
  #   settle   iteration    — the ONE write; run one tournament iteration
  #                          AND emit the proof block recording the
  #                          variety-maintenance act
  focus    target
  project  predicate
  split    candidate
  shift    basis
  settle   iteration
}

# === Kintsugi-specific actions ===
#
# Realizes mirror.spec's `command kintsugi { arg spec: ~f =
# ~f'./mirror.spec'; flag target: list(str) = []; flag emit_shatter:
# bool = false }` at the stage altitude. The action signature carries
# the same shape — a spec-file ref, an optional target whitelist, and
# the emit-shatter flag — at the substrate altitude so the dispatcher
# can walk from cli surface to settled call without leaving the
# substrate. The Rust runtime walks these declarations; it does not
# name commands. Substrate-pull discipline per
# [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]].
#
# Honest `partials` from birth (per recognition #306): the return type
# is the substrate's `imperfect` functor (declared in
# shards/glass.mirror as `imperfect(a, e, l) = success(a) |
# partial(a, l) | failure(e, l)`). A tournament round that converges
# fully returns success; a round that lifts loss without fully closing
# the gap returns partial-with-loss carrying the transparency-tagged
# residual opacity, so the kintsugi loop's next round (or a higher
# altitude) can read the unresolved fracture. Per
# [[feedback-manual-closure-is-training-pull]]: manual patching with
# opaque carriers breaks the discriminator — partial-with-loss is the
# substrate-aligned shape.

# kintsugi(spec) — run one tournament iteration against the spec at
# `spec` (defaults to `./mirror.spec` per mirror.spec's command
# declaration). Returns the imperfect carrying the settled-graph ref
# on success, the partial carrying the transparency-tagged loss on
# tolerable variety-shortfall, the failure with reason on hard error.
kintsugi(spec: ref) -> imperfect { \ }

# kintsugi_targeted(spec, target) — same as kintsugi but with an
# explicit target whitelist (matches mirror.spec's `flag target:
# list(str) = []`). When `target` is non-empty, the tournament's
# working set is restricted to the named targets (the
# variety-maintenance act runs over a sub-manifold); empty list means
# run over the spec's full settle_on set.
kintsugi_targeted(spec: ref, target: list) -> imperfect { \ }

# kintsugi_with_shatter(spec, target, emit_shatter) — full-shape entry
# matching mirror.spec's three-flag declaration. When `emit_shatter`
# is true, the iteration also writes a `.shatter` projection of the
# settled subgraph (per @mirror/lens/cli/shatter contract). This is
# the cross-stage seam: kintsugi's settle composes with shatter's
# settle when the flag is set; the two stages share the @mirror/store
# write boundary.
kintsugi_with_shatter(spec: ref, target: list, emit_shatter: bool) -> imperfect { \ }

out @mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi
out kintsugi
out kintsugi_targeted
out kintsugi_with_shatter
